WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.180 --> 00:00:05.100
It's hard to tell these days between a real movie&nbsp;
kiss and one created with visual effects trickery.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:05.100 --> 00:00:09.360
From extra cautious safety protocols to&nbsp;
the convenience of using body doubles,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:09.360 --> 00:00:12.900
here's why so many actors choose to&nbsp;
"fake it" when smooching on screen.
 &nbsp;

00:00:13.740 --> 00:00:18.540
Thor: The Dark World sees Thor's corner of the&nbsp;
MCU fall into crisis. Hundreds of years after&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:18.540 --> 00:00:22.680
the Asgardians defeated the Dark Elves,&nbsp;
Jane Foster, played by Natalie Portman,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:22.680 --> 00:00:27.000
finds a powerful weapon called the&nbsp;
Aether, which uses her as its host. Thor,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:27.000 --> 00:00:31.620
played by Chris Hemsworth, must protect her from&nbsp;
the Dark Elf Malekith before he captures her and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:31.620 --> 00:00:36.480
uses the Aether to wreak havoc on Earth. During&nbsp;
the after-credits scene, Thor returns to Earth.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:36.480 --> 00:00:41.520
With the day saved and Malekith gone, he shares&nbsp;
a passionate and celebratory kiss with Jane.

00:00:41.520 --> 00:00:46.620
Surprisingly, Hemsworth didn't actually kiss&nbsp;
Portman during this scene. She wasn't able to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:46.620 --> 00:00:51.000
make it to the reshoots, so producers decided&nbsp;
to use a body double and shoot the scene from&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:51.000 --> 00:00:55.320
behind. They gave Hemsworth a list of body&nbsp;
doubles to choose from, but Hemsworth chose his&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:55.320 --> 00:01:00.420
wife Elsa Pataky instead. She dressed up as the&nbsp;
character, ran up to him, and they locked lips.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:00.420 --> 00:01:06.720
If that isn't romantic, we don't know what is.
 
You People follows new couple Ezra and Amira as&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:06.720 --> 00:01:10.860
they deal with the cultural differences between&nbsp;
their families. After they say their wedding vows,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:10.860 --> 00:01:15.360
they lock lips, and flower petals fall around&nbsp;
them. Some might consider it the most romantic&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:15.360 --> 00:01:20.160
moment in the film — but comedian Andrew Schulz,&nbsp;
who plays Ezra's cousin Avi, revealed that the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:20.160 --> 00:01:24.840
kiss was actually created with CGI. Schulz said&nbsp;
while co-hosting the "Brilliant Idiots" podcast:

00:01:24.840 --> 00:01:29.370
"The final scene, they don't&nbsp;
even kiss. It's CGI [...]"

00:01:29.370 --> 00:01:31.860
Why didn't they go in for a real kiss? In short,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:31.860 --> 00:01:36.360
because of COVID-19 safety guidelines.&nbsp;
Filming took place in 2021, so they&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:36.360 --> 00:01:39.840
had to maintain a safe social distance to&nbsp;
help prevent the virus from spreading.  
 &nbsp;

00:01:40.380 --> 00:01:45.000
Accomplished firefighter Caleb Holt, played&nbsp;
by Kirk Cameron, realizes that his marriage&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:45.000 --> 00:01:49.260
to Catherine, played by Erin Bethea, is falling&nbsp;
apart. With the encouragement of his father,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:49.260 --> 00:01:53.940
he reads a Christian self-help book called The&nbsp;
Love Dare and embarks on its 40-day challenge&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:53.940 --> 00:01:58.140
to save his relationship. But Cameron is a&nbsp;
devout Christian, and won't kiss any woman&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:58.140 --> 00:02:03.660
besides his wife, Chelsea Noble. To film the scene&nbsp;
without compromising Cameron's values, filmmakers&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:03.660 --> 00:02:08.340
had Noble dress up as Catherine, then shot the&nbsp;
scene in silhouette. Cameron explained to Today:

00:02:08.340 --> 00:02:12.420
"The reason this movie was important&nbsp;
to me personally is because I love my&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:12.420 --> 00:02:15.840
wife dearly. We've been married&nbsp;
for 17 years ... and we have six&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:15.840 --> 00:02:19.380
children. So marriage is a very&nbsp;
special and sacred thing to us."

00:02:19.380 --> 00:02:26.940
"It is my great privilege to love and&nbsp;
cherish exclusively my beautiful wife."

00:02:26.940 --> 00:02:31.740
In the end, Fireproof affirms these beliefs:&nbsp;
Caleb and Catherine find a way to work things&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:31.740 --> 00:02:35.520
out. Lots of romantic movies feature&nbsp;
happy endings, but few incorporate&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:35.520 --> 00:02:40.860
real-world romances in such a unique way.
 
Will Smith rose to popularity as the star&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:40.860 --> 00:02:45.840
of The Fresh Prince of Bel-Air, but he proved he&nbsp;
could also take on serious fare when he accepted&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:45.840 --> 00:02:50.820
a leading role in Six Degrees of Separation. In&nbsp;
this film, a rich couple's lifestyle is disrupted&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:50.820 --> 00:02:55.980
when a mysterious man named Paul, played by Smith,&nbsp;
visits their home, claiming to be their children's&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:55.980 --> 00:03:01.200
friend. Paul uses his charms to take advantage&nbsp;
of them, but his lies are eventually exposed.

00:03:01.200 --> 00:03:05.820
Smith was committed to the role, but there was&nbsp;
just one problem: He didn't want to kiss Anthony&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:05.820 --> 00:03:10.020
Michael Hall, who played Trent, a student&nbsp;
with a crush on Paul. Right before they&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:10.020 --> 00:03:13.920
were going to film a kiss scene, Smith told&nbsp;
the director he wouldn't do it. Ultimately,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:13.920 --> 00:03:18.240
they had to use a stand-in and shoot the scene&nbsp;
from behind. Smith told Entertainment Weekly:

00:03:18.240 --> 00:03:22.740
"It was very immature on my part. I was thinking,&nbsp;
'How are my friends in Philly going to think about&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:22.740 --> 00:03:27.360
this?' I wasn't emotionally stable enough to&nbsp;
artistically commit to that aspect of the film."

00:03:27.360 --> 00:03:32.280
Though he was undoubtedly angry with himself for&nbsp;
refusing to follow through, he went on to say that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:32.280 --> 00:03:36.000
the experience was ultimately worthwhile,&nbsp;
because it brought him valuable insight.
 &nbsp;

00:03:36.660 --> 00:03:40.260
There's nothing like getting cozy under your&nbsp;
favorite blanket with a cup of hot chocolate&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:40.260 --> 00:03:44.280
and settling in to watch a romantic&nbsp;
holiday movie. But during the pandemic,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:44.280 --> 00:03:48.180
holiday movie giant Lifetime had to figure&nbsp;
out how to film kiss scenes while still&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:48.180 --> 00:03:52.920
keeping people safe. This led to an interesting&nbsp;
solution in Christmas Ever After. This movie&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:52.920 --> 00:03:57.360
follows romance novelist Izzi Simmons, who is&nbsp;
plagued with writer's block as she arrives at&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:57.360 --> 00:04:01.560
a bed and breakfast for the holidays. The&nbsp;
inn's owner, Matt, looks just like the main&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:01.560 --> 00:04:06.120
character of her books. Izzi is finally able to&nbsp;
overcome her writer's block as they fall in love.

00:04:06.120 --> 00:04:08.460
Amy Winter, head of programming at Lifetime,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:08.460 --> 00:04:12.180
discussed making the movie's kiss&nbsp;
scene happen with TheWrap, remarking:

00:04:12.180 --> 00:04:15.360
"Everybody was incredibly concerned&nbsp;
about ... making sure that we were&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:15.360 --> 00:04:19.080
following the best possible guidelines to&nbsp;
keep people safe. [...] And the greatest,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:19.080 --> 00:04:23.820
oddest solution I've seen so far is people kissing&nbsp;
through plexiglass that can be removed in post."

00:04:23.820 --> 00:04:27.900
That's just what they did for Christmas Ever&nbsp;
After. Though the behind-the-scenes photos&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:27.900 --> 00:04:32.520
might look odd, the finished kiss is just as&nbsp;
romantic as holiday movie fans could want.
 &nbsp;

00:04:33.300 --> 00:04:37.560
Call the Midwife follows the midwives,&nbsp;
nurses, and nuns of Nonnatus House as&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:37.560 --> 00:04:41.760
they help London families during&nbsp;
the 1950s and 1960s. In Season 11,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:41.760 --> 00:04:46.140
widower Matthew gets closer to Nurse&nbsp;
Trixie, and the two eventually share a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:46.140 --> 00:04:50.340
kiss. It's incredibly romantic — but things&nbsp;
were much more awkward behind the scenes,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:50.340 --> 00:04:55.020
due to COVID-19 safety protocols. Helen George,&nbsp;
who played Nurse Trixie, told RadioTimes,

00:04:55.020 --> 00:04:58.380
"We became masters at kissing&nbsp;
through Perspex. It's probably&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:58.380 --> 00:05:02.040
the most unsexy thing you could ever&nbsp;
do. A man comes along with a little&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:02.040 --> 00:05:05.760
spray and a window wipe and wipes away&nbsp;
your spit after every take. Horrific."

00:05:05.760 --> 00:05:07.080
She went on to say,

00:05:07.080 --> 00:05:12.060
"There were a few [takes]. It takes a while to&nbsp;
get there. It was so mechanical, we'd turn our&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:12.060 --> 00:05:17.160
head to the left or bring your cheek in a bit&nbsp;
or your head. Awful. Awful. So embarrassing."

00:05:17.160 --> 00:05:20.340
It's safe to say that George wasn't&nbsp;
a fan of kissing through Perspex,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:20.340 --> 00:05:24.660
but kudos to her and her co-star for powering&nbsp;
through these scenes. Their characters have&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:24.660 --> 00:05:28.680
a special bond, and fans would've been very&nbsp;
disappointed if they didn't end up kissing.
 &nbsp;

00:05:29.400 --> 00:05:33.060
Freddie is a short-lived 2000s&nbsp;
sitcom following Freddie Moreno,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:33.060 --> 00:05:36.840
played by Freddie Prinze Jr. Freddie is&nbsp;
a chef who takes in his older sister,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:36.840 --> 00:05:40.320
niece, sister-in-law, and&nbsp;
grandmother after times get tough.

00:05:40.320 --> 00:05:45.120
"Women, wear your women things.&nbsp;
I'll wear the man things."

00:05:45.120 --> 00:05:48.480
The show's opening sequence features&nbsp;
numerous Freddie clones interacting&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:48.480 --> 00:05:51.960
with many different characters: They&nbsp;
make out with women, argue with grandma,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:51.960 --> 00:05:57.420
and help Freddie's niece with her homework. Visual&nbsp;
effects supervisor Jeff Okun explained to vfxblog:

00:05:57.420 --> 00:06:01.320
"The premise here is that Freddie is all&nbsp;
things to all his family and friends. They&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:01.320 --> 00:06:04.980
wanted a main title to reflect this, so&nbsp;
[the show's creators] came up with the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:04.980 --> 00:06:09.420
idea of having Freddie continually clone off&nbsp;
into a new Freddie for EACH person's needs."

00:06:09.420 --> 00:06:13.980
To create the multiplying Freddies' smooching,&nbsp;
the visual effects crew created a mish-mash&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:13.980 --> 00:06:19.020
of partially real and partially fake kissing.&nbsp;
First, the crew filmed Prinze actually kissing&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:19.020 --> 00:06:22.980
the actress. After that, they did another&nbsp;
take with Prinze pretending to kiss her,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:22.980 --> 00:06:27.240
so he could play the part of the different&nbsp;
clone that splits off at that moment. Okun said:

00:06:27.240 --> 00:06:30.240
"We used the frozen video to&nbsp;
position him to match himself.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:30.240 --> 00:06:33.300
That way the clone would start on&nbsp;
the movement of splitting apart,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:33.300 --> 00:06:37.320
and all we needed to do was use an&nbsp;
articulated wipe to reveal the new Freddie."

00:06:37.320 --> 00:06:41.340
It took many tries to get everything lined up&nbsp;
properly, but in the end, they pulled it off.

00:06:42.060 --> 00:06:46.440
EastEnders is a long-running soap opera&nbsp;
exploring the drama-filled relationships&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:46.440 --> 00:06:49.920
between the people of London's East End.&nbsp;
And what would a soap opera be without&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:49.920 --> 00:06:53.760
plenty of kissing scenes? Filming intimate&nbsp;
moments during the pandemic was a challenge,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:53.760 --> 00:06:57.540
however. Luckily, the crew found multiple&nbsp;
ways to get around this issue while still&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:57.540 --> 00:07:02.580
following COVID-19 protocols. In 2020,&nbsp;
executive producer Jon Sen told Sky News,

00:07:02.580 --> 00:07:06.900
"For those really crucial moments where two&nbsp;
characters kiss, we've actually invited the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:06.900 --> 00:07:10.680
real-life partners of the actors on to the set&nbsp;
to be able to cheat those kind of moments."

00:07:10.680 --> 00:07:14.820
These scenes were also shot from specific&nbsp;
angles, to obscure the fact that body doubles&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:14.820 --> 00:07:18.960
were used.This wasn't the only bit of clever&nbsp;
trickery the creators of EastEnders employed.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:18.960 --> 00:07:23.220
To film scenes involving characters who&nbsp;
live together, producers used Perspex to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:23.220 --> 00:07:27.600
make close proximity COVID-safe. At other&nbsp;
times, they used plate shots, which combine&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:27.600 --> 00:07:33.480
separately-filmed actors into one coherent shot.
 
British soap opera Coronation Street follows the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:33.480 --> 00:07:38.100
working-class residents of Weatherfield, England&nbsp;
as they navigate family life, romance, work,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:38.100 --> 00:07:43.080
and various personal issues. During the pandemic,&nbsp;
producers had to figure out how to film a scene&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:43.080 --> 00:07:47.940
in which Tyrone, played by Alan Halsall,&nbsp;
and Alina, played by Ruxandra Porojnicu,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:47.940 --> 00:07:51.660
share a kiss. They managed to fake the&nbsp;
lip-lock with the help of Alex Mulvaney,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:51.660 --> 00:07:56.640
a regular extra on the show, and his wife Hannah.&nbsp;
Mulvaney told the BBC during a 2021 interview:

00:07:56.640 --> 00:08:00.720
"My agent said that 'Coronation Street'&nbsp;
needed extras [...] My partner Hannah&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:00.720 --> 00:08:05.280
hadn't done any extra work before, but my&nbsp;
agent knew she had dark hair like Alina's,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:05.280 --> 00:08:06.960
so I asked her if she wanted to do it."

00:08:06.960 --> 00:08:10.980
Despite some initial nervousness — she's&nbsp;
a big fan of the show — Hannah was up for&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:10.980 --> 00:08:14.880
it. To create the non-kissing parts of&nbsp;
the scene, Halsall and Porojnicu were&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:14.880 --> 00:08:18.120
each filmed separately. Because they&nbsp;
couldn't gaze into each other's eyes,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:18.120 --> 00:08:22.620
the actors looked at a pair of sunglasses&nbsp;
taped to a pole. Once everything was filmed,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:22.620 --> 00:08:26.640
visual effects artists brought these disparate&nbsp;
shots together into a coherent whole. 
 &nbsp;

00:08:27.180 --> 00:08:31.920
In this long-running Australian soap opera,&nbsp;
the residents of Ramsay Street face challenges&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:31.920 --> 00:08:37.200
involving work, love, their pasts, and much more.&nbsp;
Like many other shows filmed during the pandemic,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:37.200 --> 00:08:40.560
the makers of Neighbours didn't want&nbsp;
to exclude kiss scenes, leading them&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:40.560 --> 00:08:45.360
to explore unique approaches. Colette Mann, who&nbsp;
plays Sheila Canning, revealed one way they made&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:45.360 --> 00:08:50.100
the smooches happen: Each actor kissed a mirror.&nbsp;
Then the visual effects team worked their magic,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:50.100 --> 00:08:54.060
making it seem like the characters were&nbsp;
kissing each other. Smooching a mirror&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:54.060 --> 00:08:58.020
isn't exactly romantic, but hey — crazier&nbsp;
things have happened on soap operas before.

00:08:58.020 --> 00:09:02.580
For the scene in which Pierce Greyson,&nbsp;
played by Tim Robards, kisses Dipi Rebecchi,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:02.580 --> 00:09:06.780
played by Sharon Johal, the Neighbours crew&nbsp;
left the mirror behind and brought in Johal's&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:06.780 --> 00:09:10.980
husband. As Johal told Herald Sun, she even&nbsp;
made him practice the night beforehand:

00:09:10.980 --> 00:09:14.100
"I thought my kissing skills were&nbsp;
up to scratch, but obviously not."

00:09:14.100 --> 00:09:16.440
Johal's husband Ankur also remarked:

00:09:16.440 --> 00:09:20.280
"She told me it was more for blocking ... so&nbsp;
she was experimenting with different angles."

00:09:20.280 --> 00:09:24.900
The pandemic made lots of couples do weird things,&nbsp;
but this is definitely one of the strangest.
 &nbsp;

00:09:25.560 --> 00:09:31.260
Plan A Plan B centers around matchmaker Dr. Nirali&nbsp;
Vora, played by Tamannaah, who opens up a business&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:31.260 --> 00:09:35.700
next to cynical divorce lawyer Kaustubh&nbsp;
Chougule, played by Riteish Deshmukh. They&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:35.700 --> 00:09:40.140
constantly butt heads, but have so much chemistry&nbsp;
that they end up falling in love. Though Deshmukh&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:40.140 --> 00:09:44.400
refuses to do on-screen lip-locks, the two&nbsp;
characters manage to have a lengthy kiss scene.

00:09:44.400 --> 00:09:48.660
Filmmakers made this happen by using certain&nbsp;
camera angles to create the appearance of kissing,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:48.660 --> 00:09:52.320
without Deshmukh and Tamannaah ever&nbsp;
actually making contact. Some viewers&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:52.320 --> 00:09:57.600
find the faux-smooch convincing, but others think&nbsp;
it looks laughably fake. Why the deception? Fans&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:57.600 --> 00:10:01.320
speculate Deshmukh believes kisses are meant&nbsp;
to be private moments between two people.
 &nbsp;

00:10:02.100 --> 00:10:06.000
Lindsay Lohan and Charlie Sheen don't&nbsp;
play the leading roles in Scary Movie 5,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:06.000 --> 00:10:08.460
but they still have some funny&nbsp;
moments together on screen.

00:10:08.460 --> 00:10:12.304
"I'm going to blow you across&nbsp;
the room!" "Sounds awesome."

00:10:12.304 --> 00:10:14.160
Charlie brings Lindsay to a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:14.160 --> 00:10:18.540
cabin to film a racy tape. They make the&nbsp;
adults-only entertainment with gusto — but&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:18.540 --> 00:10:23.940
in truth, no actual kissing takes place. According&nbsp;
to TMZ, Lohan was supposed to kiss Sheen multiple&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:23.940 --> 00:10:28.980
times, but refused to do so. Allegedly, she made&nbsp;
this stance clear well before filming took place,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:28.980 --> 00:10:32.880
but changes weren't made until shooting&nbsp;
was set to commence. Rumor has it they&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:32.880 --> 00:10:36.540
brought in a body double and did what they&nbsp;
could to cut unnecessary kiss scenes out&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:36.540 --> 00:10:40.680
of the script entirely. Does this result in&nbsp;
a convincing scene? Your mileage may vary,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:40.680 --> 00:10:44.700
but you're probably not judging a Scary Movie&nbsp;
installment based on its convincing smooches.

